828:{"timestamp":"2026-09-19T01:32:48.047Z","ordinal":827,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a0b6cf-7404-7880-8ddd-a359958a6045","turn_id":"01a0b73c-b503-7833-acd2-10a51d3e769f","item":{"type":"AgentMessage","id":"msg_0fe2b1c4f4943f13016aade639f64087d1a41f7b3a9cb2e58e","content":[{"type":"Text","text":"Yes — the first-RHS comparison isolates it to the new periodic boundary flux kernels, not `phi` synchronization.

| First RHS quantity | `1801f4e2b` | `d06ba6594` |
|---|---:|---:|
| `gyro_phi` squared norm | 1.999999999988 | 1.999999999988 |
| `yfield` max abs | 3.13 | 149.32 |
| `cflrate` max | 80.27 | 1773.61 |

So the potential is identical initially, but `yfield` is already wrong by about 48×; its use in the `v_\parallel` flux then raises the CFL rate by 22.1×. That exactly explains the much smaller first timestep and eventual instability.

The issue is in the generated split periodic boundary kernel:
[`gk_collisionless_flux_boundary_surfx_periodic_1x2v_ser_p1.c`](gyrokinetic/ker/gk_collisionless_flux/gk_collisionless_flux_boundary_surfx_periodic_1x2v_ser_p1.c).

The old unsplit edge kernel evaluates the two traces with their respective surface traces of `bmag`:

- left: `bmag[0] + sqrt(3)*bmag[1]`
- right: `bmag[0] - sqrt(3)*bmag[1]`

The new split kernels instead use one trace for both states:

- lower periodic kernel uses the right (`-`) trace for both `ypotL_quad` and `ypotR_quad`;
- upper periodic kernel uses the left (`+`) trace for both.

That is incorrect at the periodic seam. In particular, in the lower kernel, around line 84, `ypotL_quad` needs the `+ sqrt(3)*bmag[1]` trace; in the upper kernel, around line 260, `ypotR_quad` needs the `- sqrt(3)*bmag[1]` trace. The same correction is needed at all six quadrature-node expressions in each half.

This comes from splitting the former edge kernel into lower/upper ownership kernels: ownership should decide which `yfield` array is updated, but it must not change which side’s `bmag` trace is used to evaluate each of the two states.

